---
/**
 * The use page (T-019, FR-036 – FR-040).
 *
 * The page an operator returns to daily, and the cheapest one to get wrong:
 * the panel already labels its own states, so every state name and reason line
 * below is the panel's own wording rather than a paraphrase — 005 FR-020 binds
 * this page as well as the panel. The vocabulary is **Dispatches** and
 * **Bindings** (FR-050).
 *
 * The state table is generated from the service's own filter vocabulary through
 * `DispatchStates`; the descriptions are prose and arrive as a required prop,
 * so a state the panel can display but this page cannot describe fails the
 * build rather than shipping an empty cell.
 *
 * The tracking lifecycle (002's section K, GitHub issue #13) lives here too, under
 * *What happens after a dispatch*: it is a property of a dispatched unit of work, so
 * the page that owns the dispatch owns it.
 *
 * `.astro` rather than `.md` for the reason `install.astro` sets out: a markdown
 * page cannot render the generated table.
 */
import Layout from '../layout.astro';
import DispatchStates from '../components/dispatch-states.astro';
import { withBase } from '../data/site.ts';

/**
 * What each state means and which control moves it, in the panel's words.
 *
 * Keyed by state token, not positional: `DispatchStates` resolves each token
 * against this map and throws when one is missing.
 */
const stateMeaning: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
    pending:
        'waiting for a panel — there is nothing to retry until it fails or a guard refuses it. No control is offered.',
    claimed:
        'a panel holds the lease — its result, or the lease expiring, decides what happens next. No control is offered.',
    starting:
        'a dispatch is authorized and in progress — the result or the deadline decides what happens next. No control is offered.',
    dispatched:
        'a session exists for this dispatch — a dispatched dispatch cannot be retried, and the panel offers no control.',
    failed:
        'the dispatch made no session — **Retry dispatch** returns it to waiting under the same dispatch key, with the attempt counted up.',
    unconfirmed:
        'no result arrived before the deadline, so a session may or may not exist. **Resolve dispatch** asks which: *Session was created* (with the session id, read from OpenChamber’s session list under the attachment id) or *No session was created*, which returns it to waiting. Check the session list before answering — the panel holds no record of it.',
    'dead-lettered':
        'the automatic requeue budget is spent, so nothing further happens without you. **Return to waiting** resets the attempt count and the budget, keeping the source references and every prior attempt record.',
    blocked:
        'a guard refused the dispatch before any session was started — the panel names the cause and offers **Retry dispatch**, which the service accepts only once the cause has actually cleared. See the causes below.',
};
---

<Layout title="Use">
    <h1>Use</h1>

    <p>
        Mecha Turk does its own work: it polls, it queues, it starts sessions, and it records what
        happened. This page is about watching that happen — reading a dispatch, understanding the state it
        is in, and acting on the states that wait for you.
    </p>

    <h2>Your first dispatch</h2>

    <ol>
        <li>
            Trigger the work on GitHub: assign an issue to the identity of a bound account, or request a
            review from it, or mention it where a <strong>Mention</strong> trigger is enabled on that
            binding.
        </li>
        <li>
            Within about two poll intervals, a row appears under <strong>Dispatches</strong> — two
            intervals because the scan that detects the event and the cycle that enqueues it are separate
            passes.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>host.startSession()</code> fires. OpenChamber's own harness creates the session, the
            worktree, and the chat; the extension does not create any of them. The app may switch to the
            new chat once, so the panel can read back which agent took the session.
        </li>
        <li>
            The dispatch is marked <strong>dispatched</strong> and its row carries the session that was
            created. Select the row and press <strong>Audit history</strong> to read that dispatch's whole
            trail — creation, claim, authorization, result, verification — under its correlation
            identifier, in order, from the panel alone.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>One session per dispatch, and never a second one</h2>

    <p>
        A dispatch produces <strong>at most one session</strong>. Triggers that hit the same issue
        coalesce into one dispatch, and the authorization that admits the dispatch mints a single-use
        token, so a second session for one dispatch is impossible rather than merely unlikely. A dispatch
        that already produced a session <strong>cannot be retried</strong>: the panel offers no control
        on such a row, and the service refuses a retry outright, naming the session that already exists.
    </p>

    <p>
        A dispatch that made <em>no</em> session is a different matter, and keeps its <strong>Retry dispatch</strong> control.
        A retry returns the same dispatch to waiting under the
        same key with the attempt counted up — the same unit of work, retried, rather than a new one.
    </p>

    <h2>What happens after a dispatch: following up</h2>

    <p>
        Once a work item has been dispatched, Mecha Turk keeps watching it. A comment left on the issue,
        or a new push to the pull request, is delivered as a <strong>follow-up</strong>: a prompt into the
        session the dispatch already opened. It is not a new dispatch, it opens no second session, and it
        starts no second agent on work that already has one.
    </p>

    <p>
        Delivering a follow-up moves your view: the panel asks the host to open the session the work is
        already in, and records why it is moving you before it does. A follow-up is judged exactly as the
        dispatch that started the session was — autonomously by default, with no approval step of its own.
    </p>

    <h3>What ends it, and what does not</h3>

    <p>
        Tracking ends when the item itself reaches its terminal state on GitHub: an <strong>issue
        closed</strong>, a pull request <strong>merged</strong>, or one <strong>closed unmerged</strong>.
        That is the end, and nothing else is. A session finishing does not end tracking — the item is
        still open, and the next comment on it is still a follow-up.
    </p>

    <p>
        The end is recorded as an audit row against the dispatch, naming the terminal fact and, where
        GitHub reports one, the date — so <em>when did this work item conclude?</em> is answerable
        from <strong>Audit history</strong>.
    </p>

    <h3>Waiting, and parked</h3>

    <p>
        The <strong>Status</strong> tab carries a <strong>Follow-ups</strong> block, and it reports how
        many follow-ups are <em>waiting to be delivered</em> — a queue, never delivered work. Before the
        panel’s first relay tick has counted anything it reads <em>Not counted yet this session</em>,
        rather than a zero nobody measured.
    </p>

    <p>
        Each dispatch row carries the same three facts about its own follow-ups: how many reached the
        session, how many are still waiting, and every parked one with its cause — <code>follow-ups: 2
        delivered · 1 waiting · parked: the session was mid-turn</code>. A row with no follow-up state
        carries no such line.
    </p>

    <p>
        A <strong>parked</strong> follow-up kept failing — the session was mid-turn, no session was
        open, the host refused — and has spent the bounded retry ladder the service declares. It is
        excluded from automatic handling: no later tick attempts it, and the panel carries no control
        that re-offers one, so it stays parked. Its cause is what the row and the trail keep.
    </p>

    <h3>What this did not add</h3>

    <p>
        Nothing new was installed for any of this: no new manifest capability, no new permission at the
        approval dialog, no new GitHub token scope, and no new setting. Follow-up delivery uses the
        already-declared <code>prompt</code> capability, and detecting one adds no request — it rides
        the same feeds the scan already reads.
    </p>

    <h2>What each state means, and what moves it</h2>

    <p>
        Every dispatch row carries a state and a reason line, and every state that waits for a decision
        offers the control that resolves it. Where no control applies, the control is absent rather than
        disabled, and the reason line says why.
    </p>

    <DispatchStates describe={stateMeaning} />

    <h2>Closing the panel never strands work</h2>

    <p>
        Polling and dispatch stop when OpenChamber stops — that dependency is the product's, not a gap in
        it — but a panel you merely closed does not strand anything. A claim whose lease expires returns
        to waiting on its own, with the attempt counted up and the reason audited, so the work is picked
        up rather than abandoned.
    </p>

    <p>
        The one state that waits is <strong>unconfirmed</strong>, and it is held there on purpose: a
        session may exist, because intent was reported and no result arrived before the deadline.
        It is <strong>never re-dispatched automatically</strong>. Only an explicit decision of yours — after
        checking OpenChamber’s own session list — can move it, either by recording the session that was
        created or by returning it to waiting. Automatic re-dispatch would risk a second agent session for
        work that may already be running, which is the one outcome the single-use token exists to prevent.
    </p>

    <h2>The setup prerequisites, on the Status tab</h2>

    <p>
        <strong>Status</strong> carries a <strong>Setup prerequisites</strong> section: the five things a
        first dispatch needs, each on its own line with its own state and its own remediation. The states
        are <strong>met</strong>, <strong>not met</strong>, and <strong>not checkable by the panel</strong>
        — and the third is the honest one, used where the panel genuinely cannot read the fact rather than
        displaying a verdict it never reached.
    </p>

    <p>The five, and what each state can be:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Default Agent pin</strong> — always <em>not checkable by the panel</em>, because no
            documented host API exposes that setting. Set OpenChamber’s Session Defaults → Default Agent
            to the agent you want dispatches to run on, and the matching <code>expectedAgent</code> baseline
            in Settings; the first dispatch is what checks it, and every dispatch afterwards reads the
            session back and warns when it reports another agent.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>OpenChamber running</strong> — <em>not checkable</em> until the host has answered this
            panel at all, then <em>met</em>. Polling and dispatch stop when OpenChamber closes.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Desktop or web surface</strong> — always <em>not checkable</em>. The documented host
            surface reports which kind of panel this is, never which OpenChamber app it runs in, and a
            refused service request cannot separate an unsupported surface from one that is merely not
            running. Its remediation names the consequence: VS Code and mobile do not spawn the local
            service.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>GitHub token scopes</strong> — checked fail-closed across every usable account: no
            connected account means <em>not met</em>, an account missing a required read scope means <em>not met</em>,
            an account whose scope check the panel cannot read means <em>not checkable</em>,
            and every required read scope present means <em>met</em>. No surface
            reports write scopes, so read scopes decide the state and the remediation keeps the
            no-write-scopes instruction visible.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Registered project per binding</strong> — <em>not met</em> when any binding names no
            project, with the registration routes in its remediation. No binding at all reads <em>met</em>:
            there is nothing left to resolve.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Any checkable prerequisite that is <em>not met</em> also raises a <strong>Setup prerequisites need attention</strong> notice
        above the tab strip — outside the section, so it cannot be switched away from, and naming the items and
        where their fixes live. A <em>met</em> item never raises it, and neither does a <em>not checkable</em> one:
        nagging about what the panel cannot read would teach you to ignore the banner.
    </p>

    <h2>How Status keeps itself current</h2>

    <p>
        <strong>Status</strong> is the one tab that refreshes itself. It reads again on the interval the
        last status document reported — the same interval the service is polling on — so the numbers on
        screen are not frozen at the moment you opened the panel. The tab states that period in words
        beside <strong>Refresh status</strong>, so a page whose values move on their own never does it
        without saying how often.
    </p>

    <p>
        The period follows the service rather than the panel: change the poll interval and the next read
        carries the new one, so the tab moves with it. Until that first read has landed the tab says it
        is <strong>not refreshing itself</strong> rather than showing a plausible-looking number nobody
        reported — the panel holds no default for one. The tick belongs to <strong>Status</strong> alone,
        so switching to another tab stops it, and switching back reads straight away instead of waiting
        out what was left of the old period. <strong>Refresh status</strong> is there whenever you want a
        read now, and a read that fails keeps the last document and marks it stale rather than blanking
        the tab with a summary built from nothing.
    </p>

    <p>
        When a dispatch does not go through, the <a href={withBase('/debug/')}>debug page</a> matches the
        symptom string to a cause and an action.
    </p>
</Layout>
